privacy-title = Privacy notice
privacy-lead = What this site and Baylee Central process about you, why, on which legal basis and for how long.
privacy-updated = As of 26 November 2026
privacy-controller-title = Who is responsible
privacy-controller = The controller under the GDPR is the private person named in the <impressum>legal notice</impressum>, which gives their name, postal address and e-mail address. You can also send privacy questions through the contact form. There is no data protection officer, as none is required.
privacy-short-title = In short
privacy-short = No tracking, no advertising, no analytics, no cookie unless you sign in, and nothing loaded from other servers on public pages: fonts, pictures and scripts all come from baylee.app. Only when signed in to chat with card previews enabled (switchable at any time), your browser loads card images directly from Scryfall with no referrer. Your password never reaches us, and your chat messages and private data are encrypted in your browser before they are sent.
privacy-hosting-title = Visiting the site
privacy-hosting = The site runs on a server we rent from netcup GmbH in Germany. When you open a page, your browser sends what every request carries: your IP address, the address asked for, the time, your browser's language and its identification (user agent). The server needs this to answer. It picks the language version from the language, and the download page uses the user agent to suggest the right file. The web server keeps no access log, so these requests are not stored. The server's own operating messages (its system journal) can, in rare error cases, contain an IP address. They are deleted after 14 days at the latest. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest is delivering the site safely and reliably).
privacy-views-title = Counting page views
privacy-views = The website counts how often it is visited: one single number for all pages and all visitors together, shown on the start page. What is counted is a visit: a browser arriving at the website adds one. Going on from page to page, or loading a page again, is not counted again. The server reads that from the single request (whether it came from one of our own pages, and whether it is a reload). Nothing about you is stored for this: no IP address, no cookie, no identifier, not even which page it was.
privacy-device-title = What your browser keeps
privacy-device = This site sets no cookie until you sign in. What your browser keeps on your device, and only with JavaScript: your colour choice if you made one (<code>baylee-site-theme</code>); while you are signed in, when your session ends and a random marker that detects sign-in changes (<code>baylee-central-session</code>), your display name, your tag and the key your profile picture is fetched by, so that the header can show who is signed in (<code>baylee-central-own</code>, removed when you sign out, or the next time the site is opened after your session has ended), your chat key and your contacts' trusted keys (see Chat); if you turned on notifications, a random number for this device (<code>baylee-push-installation-v1</code>, <code>baylee-push</code>); and, only once you have signed in, installed the site as an app or turned on notifications, a copy of the site's public files (page shell, fonts, scripts), so that it starts without a connection and notifications can arrive. If none of the three applies any more, the copy is removed the next time you open the site. None of this is sent to us unless a section below says so. The chat and session entries are deleted when you sign out. You remove everything else by clearing the site's data in your browser. Legal basis: § 25(2) no. 2 TDDDG (strictly necessary for the function you use), and for the notifications' random number, your consent (§ 25(1) TDDDG).
privacy-device-manager = If you keep your recovery code in your browser's password manager, mail it to yourself, save it as a file or print it on the step that shows it, your browser, your mail program, your files or your paper keep it, never this site: the code is made in your browser and never sent to us. Clearing the site's data does not remove it from there.
privacy-github-title = GitHub
privacy-github = The download page and the start page show the newest release, the test status and the card numbers. Our server asks GitHub for them, not your browser. GitHub learns nothing about you from that. Only when you click a download or GitHub link does your browser connect to GitHub (GitHub, Inc., USA) and send it your IP address and what every request carries. GitHub's own privacy notice then applies.
privacy-directory-title = The gateway directory
privacy-directory = Our server reads the directory of gateways from Baylee Central and shows it. Nothing about you is involved. A gateway may tell Central how many players are connected at the moment, how many of them are in a game, and how many open tables and games it has, and Central counts its accounts. The site shows these numbers. They are counts only, never names, and a gateway's numbers are its operator's own statement.
privacy-account-title = Your account
privacy-account = For an account, Baylee Central stores: your account id, your sign-in name, your display name with its tag, when and how the account was created, an OPAQUE password record (which lets you prove your password without our server ever learning it), your master key twice encrypted (by your password and by your recovery code), your encrypted account document and your encrypted objects (unreadable to us, as we see only size, revision and time), which version of the terms you accepted and when, the time of your last sign-in, your sessions, an administrator role if you have one, and the gateways you register with their tokens. Other players see your display name and tag, never your sign-in name. If an administrator gives you a reset code, we store only its digest until the code is used or has expired. What administrators do to an account or gateway is kept in a record for three years (who, what, which account or gateway, when). Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR (the account you asked for), and for the time of the last sign-in and the record, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest in secure, traceable operation). Without a sign-in name and a password there is no account, and you need no e-mail address. You may create an account from the age of 13. Kept until you delete the account. It is then removed from the running system at once, and from backups within 12 months at the latest. Sessions end after 30 minutes without use and at the latest 12 hours after signing in.
privacy-email-title = E-mail address
privacy-email = An e-mail address is optional. An account needs none. If you give one, Baylee Central stores the address, the language its mails are written in and when you confirmed it. An address you named that waits for its code is kept until you confirm or withdraw it, and at most a day after its code has expired. Of every code we mail, we store only a digest, until the code is used or has expired, and for 24 hours the times reset codes were mailed to you, so that no more than three a day go out. We use the address for four things: to send the code that confirms it, to send a reset code when you or anyone who knows your sign-in name asks for one, to tell the old address when it is replaced or removed, and to tell the confirmed address when a reset code set a new password, when the password was changed or when an administrator made a reset code for the account. The mail is sent by Baylee's own mail service on the same server through mailbox.org (Heinlein Hosting GmbH, Berlin), which acts as a processor and receives the address and the mail. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR (the account you asked for). Kept until you remove the address or delete the account.
privacy-cookie-title = The session cookie
privacy-cookie = Signing in sets one cookie, <code>__Host-central</code>: a random number that identifies your session. It is sent only to baylee.app, cannot be read by scripts and ends with the session, after 12 hours at the latest. It is strictly necessary to keep you signed in, and it needs no consent. Legal basis: § 25(2) no. 2 TDDDG and Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR.
privacy-chat-title = Chat and contacts
privacy-chat = Messages are encrypted on your device for each recipient's devices. Baylee Central stores them only in encrypted form and cannot read them. What Central can see and stores: your contacts, contact requests and blocks, the groups with their names, members and invitations, who sent whom a message when and how large it is, and your devices' public keys. Your contacts and the members of your groups see your display name with its tag and your messages. While the Baylee app is signed in and connected to Central, your friends (contacts you both accepted) see that you are online, recognisable by your tag (the code after your display name), and whether you are away, playing (in a game that is running, online or against the house AI) or sitting at a table whose game has not begun, but not what you play, and where only if you share your table (a setting of the gateway you play on, on until you turn it off): then also the gateway's address, the game's identifier there, the table's name and how many of its seats are taken, never for a table with a password. Nobody else sees it, neither pending requests nor blocked accounts. In the app you choose whether your friends see you online, away or offline. With “offline” they never see you online. You also appear away after ten minutes without input. On the same connection Central tells the app that something about your chats, contacts or groups changed. No content travels on it. Whether you are online and what you are doing, Central keeps only in memory while the connection is open. It is neither stored nor logged. Your choice (online, away, offline) Central stores with your account until you change it or delete the account. Your browser keeps your chat key (sealed under a key only this browser holds, in its storage <code>baylee-chat</code>) and the keys of your contacts that you trusted (encrypted, <code>baylee-chat-trust</code>) while you are signed in. Messages and deliveries are kept until you delete your account. There is no shorter automatic retention period yet. Each sender and each device holds at most 5,000 messages. After that, new ones are refused. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR.
privacy-cards-title = Card search and card images in chat
privacy-cards = When you type # in signed-in chat, Baylee Central searches its own catalogue imported from Scryfall bulk data, strictly in your selected language and English, for matching cards and printings. While card previews are enabled in chat (on by default while signed in; your choice baylee-chat-card-previews is kept only locally in your browser and can be turned off at any time in chat), hovering, focusing or tapping a card loads its image directly from Scryfall (cards.scryfall.io, without referrer or cookies, unaltered with artist credit and excluding Universes Beyond prints). Your browser necessarily sends your IP address to Scryfall (which may serve images via a CDN outside the EEA). Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR (requested chat feature) and § 25(2) no. 2 TDDDG for your local toggle preference.
privacy-push-title = Notifications
privacy-push = If you turn on notifications in a browser, your browser registers with its push service, and Central stores that service's address for this browser, the browser's public key and authentication secret, your language, a random number for this device and your choice of categories. The push service belongs to your browser's maker: Google (Firebase Cloud Messaging), Mozilla or Apple, all based in the USA. A notification carries, encrypted, only its kind (for example "new message") and your language, never names or text. The push service sees connection data such as address, time and size. These details are deleted when you turn notifications off on this device, when you sign out, or when you delete your account. An address not renewed for 90 days is no longer used, and waiting notifications expire after one day. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR and § 25(1) TDDDG (your consent). You can withdraw it at any time in your account's settings. What happened until then stays lawful.
privacy-counters-title = Protection against abuse
privacy-counters = To stop password guessing and overload, Central counts requests per IP address (registrations, sign-in attempts, password resets, reading the directory and, more generally, how many requests an address sends within a minute), only in memory: registrations, and sign-in attempts that were not completed with the right password, for up to a day, everything else for at most one hour. After a sign-in with the right password, Central remembers for 30 days, again only in memory, that your account signed in from that address (an IPv4 address, or the first half of an IPv6 address — at most eight per account), so that other people's attempts on your account do not keep you out there. Someone who uses the same address and knows your user name can tell from Central's answers that your account signed in from it. A new password forgets these addresses except the one you set it from, deleting the account forgets them all, and so does a restart of the service. Before a registration your browser also solves a puzzle that takes memory and computing time. It consists of random numbers, holds nothing about you and is forgotten after twenty minutes at the latest. Central computes a small part of it again to check your browser's answer. How hard it is depends on how many accounts are being created and on whether the request looks like a program's: whether it carries the headers every browser sends, whether your browser says a program is driving it, and how many puzzles your address and the network block around it (the first three numbers of an IPv4 address, the first 48 bits of an IPv6 address) asked for in the last hour. Of this Central keeps only the counts, in memory, for at most an hour. Per account, Central counts contact requests and searches for other players, so that nobody floods others with requests. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest is protecting the accounts and the service against abuse).
privacy-contact-title = The contact form
privacy-contact = The form sends your message, the page's language and the time to Baylee's feedback service on the same server, and your name and e-mail address only if you give them. Both are optional. The service also notes whether the message has been dealt with. Your IP address is not stored. It is only counted in memory for one hour, to stop a flood of messages. Only the person named in the legal notice reads the messages, and nothing is passed on. A message is deleted 90 days after it was dealt with, after one year at the latest, or earlier if you ask. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest in answering messages), or (b) where your message concerns your account.
privacy-impressum-title = The legal notice's puzzle
privacy-impressum = The legal notice is shown only after your browser has solved a puzzle. That protects the address from programs that collect addresses. The puzzle consists of random numbers and holds nothing about you. For this, the feedback service keeps in memory only, never on disk and never in a log: how many puzzles your IP address asked for (for one hour — an IPv6 address by its first half), whether it followed a link that is hidden from people (for 24 hours), and which puzzles were solved (for five minutes, without an address). Three yes-or-no answers about your browser set the puzzle's difficulty and are not kept: whether a program drives it, whether it names no languages, and whether it draws without a graphics card. Nobody is refused. A browser that looks like a program gets a longer puzzle. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest in protecting a private person's address).
privacy-recipients-title = Recipients and countries
privacy-recipients = We sell no data and pass none on for advertising. Recipients are: netcup GmbH (hosting in Germany, as a processor under Art. 28 GDPR); mailbox.org (Heinlein Hosting GmbH, Berlin), which delivers the mails to an e-mail address you gave, as a processor; Baylee Central's administrators, who for administration see your sign-in name, your display name with its tag, your account's origin, creation and last sign-in, and your gateways, never your encrypted data; a gateway you sign in to with your account (it receives your account id, display name and tag, not your password); your contacts and the members of your groups (display name, tag and your messages), and your friends, who see your status (online, away, playing, at a table) while the Baylee app is connected to Central and you did not choose “offline”; Scryfall (cards.scryfall.io), if you keep card previews enabled in signed-in chat (direct browser image load without referrer or cookies); authorities only where the law obliges us. Countries outside the European Economic Area: only when you turn on notifications does your browser's push service (Google, Mozilla or Apple, USA) carry them, on the basis of the European Commission's adequacy decision on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, as far as the provider is certified under it. Scryfall may also deliver card images from servers outside the EEA; you can switch off card previews in chat at any time. Gateways run by others can be in any country. Whether you sign in there is your decision. Otherwise we transfer no data to countries outside the European Economic Area.
privacy-rights-title = Your rights
privacy-rights = You have the right of access (Art. 15 GDPR), rectification (Art. 16), erasure (Art. 17), restriction of processing (Art. 18) and data portability (Art. 20). You can object at any time, on grounds relating to your particular situation, to processing based on our legitimate interests under Art. 6(1)(f) (Art. 21). You can withdraw a consent at any time for the future (Art. 7(3)). If you are under 16, a consent counts only with the agreement of your parents or other guardians (Art. 8). Your account's data can be exported and the account deleted in your account's settings. For everything else, a message through the contact form or to the address in the legal notice is enough. You may lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority (Art. 77), for example the one where you live or the State Commissioner for Data Protection and Freedom of Information of North Rhine-Westphalia. No law or contract obliges you to give us data. Without a sign-in name and a password, however, there is no account. There is no automated decision-making and no profiling (Art. 22).
privacy-objection-title = Your right to object
privacy-objection = Where we process data on the basis of Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (delivering the site, protection against abuse, the contact form, the legal notice's puzzle, the time of your last sign-in, the administration record, bug reports and the usage count), you can object at any time on grounds relating to your particular situation (Art. 21 GDPR). We then stop processing the data, unless we can show compelling legitimate grounds that override your interests, or the processing serves to establish, exercise or defend legal claims. An informal message through the contact form or to the address in the legal notice is enough.
privacy-links-title = Links to other services
privacy-links = The site links to GitHub, Discord and WhatsApp, among others. These are plain links: nothing is embedded, and until you click one, none of these services learns of your visit. When you click, your browser does not say which page you came from (no referrer). After that, each provider's own privacy notice applies, and they may process data outside the EU. What other members of the community groups see of you there is decided by those services.
privacy-reports-title = Bug reports and the usage count from the game
privacy-reports = A bug report you send from the game with F8 holds its kind, your text, the client version and the attachments you leave ticked. The contact page lists them. It is deleted 90 days after it was dealt with, after three years at the latest. Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (our legitimate interest in a game without bugs). The client sends crash reports only if you allowed them (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR, and you can withdraw this at any time in the client's settings). During an offline game the client sends a random identifier of that game once a minute and nothing else. We keep it in memory only and forget it 150 seconds after its last sign of life (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, and it can be switched off under "Send anonymous usage count"). As with every connection, your IP address reaches us.
terms-title = Terms of use
terms-draft = These terms are a draft and not yet in force.

